# Global agent instructions

Classify the request first. Keep one objective, use the narrowest primary
skill, and preserve unrelated changes.

## Workflow

- Analysis and review are read-only. Implementation writes happen only in the
  canonical isolated worktree from `core/policies/worktrees.md`.
- Every write task is an implementation unit, including direct-mode work. Bind
  `delivery` before the first patch or autofix with a base SHA,
  absolute worktree and branch, exact scope, and integration authority.
- Paths under `core/policies/` resolve from the canonical agent-config root
  containing this file, not from the product repository checkout.
- Choose one primary skill and only the complementary skills that add a
  concrete discipline. Use `core/policies/routing.md` for the workflow matrix
  and project overlays for local facts.
- Before planning or implementation, apply the plan-readiness gate in the
  routing policy. Resolve material gaps with `grill-with-docs`; do not guess.
- Before any write, run the `delivery` worktree and target guards with
  absolute paths; require `PASS` before a patch, autofix, or generator.
- Skills resolve to `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` under the agent-config root.
  When multiple roots are configured or precedence is ambiguous, use
  `${AGENT_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.agents}/scripts/skill-path <name>` to resolve
  the correct path; catalog aliases such as `r0` are labels, not filesystem
  paths.
- Before implementation or delivery, load `delivery` and its project overlay;
  use its contract before writes and its gates before build, test, lint,
  validation, or release commands.
- When creating, changing, reviewing, or running tests, load `test-hygiene`;
  its checklist governs user-visible windows, browser tabs, previews,
  simulators, and other UI artifacts.
- Direct mode is the default, but it does not bypass the delivery contract,
  worktree gate, scope check, review, or validation. Delegate only broad,
  independent work with one owner, one absolute worktree, and a compact
  evidence handoff.
- Backlog, plan migration, and durable issue publication follow their named
  policies; never silently replace provider state with repository documents.
- Before changing a skill, consult `core/policies/skill-provenance.md`.
- Personal desktop/mobile work is local by default; use current platform APIs
  unless the project documents older support, and do not add CI/CD without a
  request.

## Routes

- Native macOS/AppKit/SwiftUI: use `macos-ui` for platform-native visual
  precedence, app-shell integration, and accessibility; use `swiftui-design-skill` when the UI feels generic or needs a
  deliberate visual direction; use `swiftui-design-principles` for native
  spacing/typography/colors/cards and WidgetKit polish. Prefer one primary
  design skill; do not load both `swiftui-design-skill` and
  `swiftui-design-principles` unless direction and execution are both in
  scope. Keep engineering in `swiftui-expert-skill`.
- Chrome extension, Manifest V3, `chrome.*`, content scripts, service workers,
  or side panels: use `chrome-extensions`; add web UI skills only when needed.
- Product web UI: use the focused `better-*` skills; use `interface-review` for
  a holistic review and `ux-writing` for standalone interface copy.
- Tests: use `test-hygiene` for test creation, changes, review, and execution;
  add `tdd` when test-first development or test design is in scope.
- Code review: choose `code-review` for standards/spec or
  `thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review` for maintainability risk.
- Audits and improvement plans use `improve`; end-to-end delivery uses
  `ship-ship`; architecture improvement uses `codebase-design` vocabulary.

## Safety and integrity

- Never persist prompts, transcripts, credentials, secrets, or machine state.
  Never print secret values. Keep provider behavior in adapters.
- Third-party skills remain unchanged; put local behavior in overlays or a
  first-party skill. Durable issue publication follows its provider contract;
  never fall back silently to repository documents.
- Preserve unrelated work. Do not merge, push, amend, or run destructive Git
  operations without explicit authorization. Keep implementation, review,
  merge, push, and main-validation states separate.
- An unexpected changed path is a STOP condition. Report it and preserve it;
  do not restore, delete, or absorb it into the current unit.

## Validation

- Select the smallest deterministic changed-surface gate and report its exact
  result, manual checks, unavailable checks, baseline failures, and next action.
- In this checkout, run `make validate` after changing instructions, policies,
  skills, scripts, or projections. Run `make doctor` when routing or projection
  behavior changes; report projection drift separately.

## Output style

The reader has ADHD. Shape every response so it can be acted on:

1. Lead with the answer or next action: command, path, or snippet first.
2. Number multi-step work; one bounded action per step.
3. End with one next action doable in under two minutes.
4. Finish the current issue before raising a new one.
5. Restate progress each turn ("step 3 of 5 done").
6. Give time estimates in concrete units, never "a bit".
7. After a change, show what now works.
8. Errors: state location, cause, and fix. No drama.
9. Cap lists at 5 items.
10. No preamble, no recaps, no closers.

Exceptions: explain fully when asked to explain. Confirm before destructive actions. After three failed fixes, stop and name the doubtful assumption. If the request is ambiguous, ask one short question.

